---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerFileSample from '../components/AnswerFileSample.astro';
import BootLanes from '../components/BootLanes.astro';
import ProfileExample from '../components/ProfileExample.astro';
import Status from '../components/Status.astro';
import { checkedOn, sourceRevision } from '../data/boot-chain';

const checked = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { dateStyle: 'long', timeZone: 'UTC' }).format(
  new Date(`${checkedOn}T00:00:00Z`),
);

const repo = 'https://github.com/kyro-pxe/kyro';
const roadmap = `${repo}/blob/main/docs/ROADMAP.md`;
const checkedRoadmap = `${repo}/blob/${sourceRevision}/docs/ROADMAP.md`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Kyro: open-source PXE server for Windows 11"
  description="Kyro is an open-source PXE server for unattended Windows 11 installs. The alpha has completed one QEMU installation; physical hardware is untested."
>
  <div class="frame">
    <div class="hero">
      <h1>Install Windows&nbsp;11 <span>over the network.</span></h1>
      <div class="pair pair-top">
        <div class="hero-text">
          <p class="lede">
            Give Kyro a Windows 11 ISO and an answer file, then arm a client for one installation.
            Kyro serves Windows Setup over the network from a single Linux program. No USB stick,
            no unpacking the ISO by hand.
          </p>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="button" href="/generator/">Create an answer file</a>
            <a class="text-link" href={`${repo}#quick-start`}>Set up Kyro</a>
          </div>
          <Status state="tested" block>
            Alpha: one complete installation in QEMU with OVMF, Secure Boot off, and the
            Windows 11 Enterprise evaluation ISO. Physical hardware is untested.
          </Status>
        </div>
        <div>
          <BootLanes />
          <p class="small chart-note">
            Test scope and statuses checked against the <a href={checkedRoadmap}>repository on {checked}</a>.
            See the <a href={roadmap}>current roadmap</a> for changes and remaining work.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="section pair pair-top" aria-labelledby="why">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="why">The boot services, together in one program.</h2>
        <p>
          Kyro combines proxyDHCP, TFTP, HTTP and a read-only SMB server in one Go binary.
          Your existing DHCP server still assigns addresses; Kyro supplies the boot information
          alongside it. An existing DHCP server is required.
        </p>
        <p>
          It reads files directly from your Windows ISO without mounting or unpacking it.
          iPXE and wimboot are downloaded from their upstream projects, used unmodified and
          checked against pinned checksums. You supply your own Windows ISO and licenses.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 id="facts">Current alpha and its limits</h3>
        <dl class="facts" aria-labelledby="facts">
          <div><dt>License</dt><dd>GPL, version 3 or later</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Form</dt><dd>One static binary, written in Go</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Server</dt><dd>Linux x86-64; client boots in Docker are untested</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Clients</dt><dd>UEFI x86-64, Secure Boot off; QEMU only so far</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Windows</dt><dd>One Enterprise evaluation ISO tested</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Manage</dt><dd>CLI and a read-only status page</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Pending</dt><dd>BIOS boots, Secure Boot, NIC driver injection and Windows servers</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="small chart-note"><a href={`${repo}#known-limitations`}>Read all known limitations</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section pair" aria-labelledby="profiles">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="profiles">One ISO, any number of setups.</h2>
        <p>
          Keep a Windows ISO once and reuse it with different answer files. An image holds the
          installation media; a profile holds an answer file and its metadata.
        </p>
        <p>
          Import a profile, then arm a client by MAC address, UUID or serial number with the
          image and profile to use. Unarmed clients and later network boots are sent to the local
          disk. Installing again requires another arming.
        </p>
        <p>
          A profile that wipes a disk requires <code>--confirm-wipe</code> when arming. Review
          the answer file first: selecting a client is not authentication.
        </p>
        <p>
          <Status state="tested">Profile import and arming are tested. An installation selection menu is still planned.</Status>
        </p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0002-profiles.md`}>Read how profiles work</a></p>
      </div>
      <ProfileExample />
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="generator">
    <div class="frame pair">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="generator">Answer files, written in your browser.</h2>
        <p>
          Windows Setup asks for a language, a disk, an account and more. An answer file
          (<code>autounattend.xml</code>) answers in advance, so nobody has to sit in front of the
          installer.
        </p>
        <p>
          The generator writes Windows 11 answer files and Kyro profiles in your browser.
          What you type, passwords included, stays on your computer. The page forbids its
          scripts from making network connections.
        </p>
        <p>
          Download <code>autounattend.xml</code> or a profile for the current Kyro alpha
          (provisional bundle version 0). Save a separate settings file to edit your form later.
        </p>
        <p>
          <Status state="working">The generator is available. Windows installation testing is still pending.</Status>
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button button-outline" href="/generator/">Create an answer file</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <AnswerFileSample />
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="frame">
    <section class="section pair pair-top" aria-labelledby="security">
      <div class="copy">
        <h2 id="security">Made for a boot network you control.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          PXE has no authentication, and Kyro cannot change that. The security model says so
          before anything else.
        </p>
        <p><a class="text-link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/security-model.md`}>Read the security model</a></p>
      </div>
      <ul class="points">
        <li>
          <h3>Anyone on the network can answer.</h3>
          <p>
            A booting PC acts on the DHCP answers it gets. Any machine in the same broadcast
            domain can point it at a different boot file.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Give it its own VLAN.</h3>
          <p>
            Kyro belongs on an isolated boot VLAN that contains only Kyro and the machines being
            installed.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Answer files are readable there.</h3>
          <p>
            Kyro serves the answer file during an armed installation over unencrypted HTTP.
            Anyone who can read the traffic sees its passwords. A machine can also claim an
            armed client's identifiers. Keep long-lived secrets out of answer files.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    display: grid;
    gap: clamp(2rem, 1.25rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);
    padding-block-start: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 5vw, 5rem);
  }

  /* The second half of the headline starts its own line where there is room. */
  @media (min-width: 46rem) {
    h1 span {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  h1 span {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .hero-text {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    justify-items: start;
  }

  .chart-note {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
  }

  .facts {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-3);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .facts div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 6.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .facts dt {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .facts dd {
    color: var(--strong);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .points {
    padding: 0;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--line);
    list-style: none;
  }

  .points li {
    padding-block: var(--space-5);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .points p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
  }
</style>
